From: Artur Andrzejczak <andrzejczak.artur@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 21 Sep 2026 00:30:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] ggml-cuda: RDNA1 prefill GEMM: the mixture-of-experts path

MUL_MAT_ID reaches ggml_cuda_mul_mat_id, not ggml_cuda_mul_mat, so the packed-
fp16 GEMM never saw a mixture-of-experts model's experts: they stayed on MMQ and
a kernel trace puts them at 60 percent of qwen3.6-35B-A3B's prefill, running at
1.18 TFLOP/s where the chip does 14.4 in packed fp16.

ggml_cuda_launch_mm_ids_helper already builds everything needed, and MMQ and MMF
both call it: a compact ordering of the used (token, slot) pairs sorted by
expert, the activation column each compact slot reads, the destination column it
writes, and each expert's range. This runs the same GEMM over that ordering, one
expert per block in z, walking that expert's column tiles in a loop so the grid
does not have to be sized for the most heavily used expert. No activations are
gathered into a compact buffer: the column index is read through the map.

The column tile is 32 here against 64 on the dense path. At a 512-token batch
with eight of 256 experts used an expert sees about sixteen columns, so 64 is
mostly padding, and 16 amortises the weight dequantisation over too few outputs.
32 measured ahead of both at every batch: against MMQ it is 1.75 times at 256
tokens, 1.55 at 512 and 1.55 at 2048, taking the model's pp512 from 379 to 588
against Vulkan's 457.

Accuracy improves instead of degrading, for the reason it did when the kernel
first shipped on the dense path: the experts are IQ2_XXS and IQ3_XXS, far
narrower than the eight bits MMQ quantises the activations to, so fp16
activations are the better trade. Six chunks of wiki.test.raw at context 512
read 6.2265 against MMQ's 6.2473, and greedy text over 64 tokens is identical.
test-backend-ops passes 865/865 MUL_MAT_ID cases at all three column widths.

GGML_RDNA1_PKF16_ID=0 puts the experts back on MMQ and
GGML_RDNA1_PKF16_ID_BN picks 16, 32 or 64. The kernel now carries a static
assertion for the tile constraint it always had, that a block has exactly BM
threads and therefore BN / TN must equal TM; getting that wrong is how the first
version of this patch failed 24 of the 865 cases, leaving some columns unwritten.

Co-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Artur Andrzejczak <andrzejczak.artur@gmail.com>
